WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.160 --> 00:00:08.080
What’s up guys?

00:00:08.090 --> 00:00:09.180
Jared again.

00:00:09.320 --> 00:00:13.959
After seeing how anime studio Madhouse knocked
season 2 of Mob Psycho 100 right out of the

00:00:13.959 --> 00:00:18.130
park, I, along with most, was pretty stoked
to see what they would do with everyone’s

00:00:18.130 --> 00:00:26.759
favorite OP superhero, Saitama.Or as he’s
known in Season 2, Bald Cape.

00:00:26.759 --> 00:00:31.680
But somewhere between the middling Episode
1 and where we are now, I came to the conclusion

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:35.750
that One-Punch Man just flat out isn’t delivering
in its second outing.

00:00:35.750 --> 00:00:40.700
Sure, the animation quality has taken an unfortunate
hit, but there are still some good fight scenes

00:00:40.700 --> 00:00:42.190
and a few laughs as well.

00:00:42.190 --> 00:00:55.820
But ultimately, where Season 2 fails is exactly
what made Season 1 such a success: its adherence

00:00:55.820 --> 00:00:58.590
to the formal conventions of anime.

00:00:58.590 --> 00:01:02.510
Now before you nuke this video to oblivion,
I’m not saying that Season 1 wasn’t a

00:01:02.510 --> 00:01:05.110
critique of the shonen genre – it was.

00:01:05.110 --> 00:01:10.000
But for all One-Punch Man’s riffing on series
like Dragon Ball Z and Naruto, Season 1 remained

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:13.630
sharp because it still followed a basic shonen
formula.

00:01:13.630 --> 00:01:16.119
Season 2, though… not so much.

00:01:16.119 --> 00:01:20.609
So join me for this Wisecrack Edition on One-Punch
Man Season 2: What Went Wrong?

00:01:20.609 --> 00:01:27.960
And, obviously, spoilers ahead.

00:02:37.400 --> 00:02:41.440
Alright guys, a quick recap of what’s going
on with our bald crusader is in order.

00:02:41.440 --> 00:02:44.920
Saitama lives in a world populated by heroes
and monsters.

00:02:44.920 --> 00:02:49.170
After being royally rejected for an entry
level job, Saitama stumbles upon this deeply

00:02:49.170 --> 00:02:55.060
unfortunate-looking child who Sharpied nipples
on this equally unfortunate-looking monster.

00:02:55.060 --> 00:02:59.220
Remembering how he once wanted to be a hero,
Saitama decides to protect our ugly youth

00:02:59.220 --> 00:03:01.900
and disembowels our crab-based baddie.

00:03:01.900 --> 00:03:11.770
Three years of training later and the results
are clear: .

00:03:11.770 --> 00:03:14.790
But with all this power comes immense boredom.

00:03:14.790 --> 00:03:18.330
Whether it’s training his new disciple,
Genos, or climbing up the ranks of the Hero

00:03:18.330 --> 00:03:23.940
Association, or even defeating a world-ending
threat like Boros, Saitama’s sheer strength

00:03:23.940 --> 00:03:26.900
saps the fun out of life.

00:03:26.900 --> 00:03:34.350
As such, a lot of Saitama’s problems in
the show don’t revolve around getting stronger,

00:03:34.350 --> 00:03:36.510
but rather navigating everyday life.

00:03:36.510 --> 00:03:39.800
And, this is where One-Punch Man leverages
a lot of its humor.

00:03:39.800 --> 00:03:44.780
For example, while every character and their
mom loves to monologue on their novel origin

00:03:44.780 --> 00:03:49.760
story, Saitama’s insane strength comes from
a mediocre exercise routine of 100 push-ups,

00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:53.010
100 sit ups, 100 squats… you know the drill.

00:03:53.010 --> 00:03:57.060
In moments like these, the show parodies the
traditional Shonen trope of heroes and villains

00:03:57.060 --> 00:04:02.360
one-upping each other through a constant slew
of new powers, like Goku going Super Saiyan,

00:04:02.360 --> 00:04:06.690
or Sasuke gaining the Mangekyou Sharingan,
or, whatever they do in Bleach.

00:04:06.690 --> 00:04:09.910
Instead, Saitama’s battles often end in
an anti-climax.

00:04:09.910 --> 00:04:15.350
We’re not watching All Might or Deku overcome
their weakness and deliver a devastating finishing

00:04:15.350 --> 00:04:16.350
blow.

00:04:16.350 --> 00:04:20.100
Instead we’re watching Saitama freak out
midfight about a bargain sale at the local

00:04:20.100 --> 00:04:33.300
super market
And, even without a gag, it always ends with

00:04:33.300 --> 00:04:35.249
a single punch.

00:04:35.249 --> 00:04:39.379
Season 2 largely continues this trend with
a few new plot elements.

00:04:39.379 --> 00:04:42.919
Saitama is still climbing the ranks of the
Hero Association, but with the self-proclaimed

00:04:42.919 --> 00:04:47.409
hero hunter, Garo, on the loose, Saitama has
entered a martial arts competition to learn

00:04:47.409 --> 00:04:48.949
how to defeat him.

00:04:48.949 --> 00:05:03.639
Never mind the fact that Saitama does exactly
that here … and here . All the while, a

00:05:03.639 --> 00:05:07.790
newly minted Monsters Association is trying
to create a world where their king, Orochi,

00:05:07.790 --> 00:05:11.580
reigns supreme, creating absolute chaos in
the process.

00:05:11.580 --> 00:05:16.669
So if Season 2 has all the same trappings
of Season 1 along with a new cast of enemies,

00:05:16.669 --> 00:05:19.050
what’s actually going wrong here?

00:05:19.050 --> 00:05:23.020
To answer that, we’re going to have to look
at what makes the shonen genre tick.

00:05:23.020 --> 00:05:27.650
As the history of anime will show, overpowered
protagonists are nothing new.

00:05:27.650 --> 00:05:31.860
Just look at the breakout hit this year, That
Time I Got Reincarnated as a Slime, which

00:05:31.860 --> 00:05:37.669
focuses on a salary man who dies only to be
reborn in a fantasy world as a super stacked

00:05:37.669 --> 00:05:38.669
slime.

00:05:38.669 --> 00:05:42.570
Seriously, this thing can absorb any monster
and gain their power, which is the definition

00:05:42.570 --> 00:05:43.570
of OP.

00:05:43.570 --> 00:05:48.479
But when your hero is already the world’s
strongest, you have yourself a problem.

00:05:48.479 --> 00:05:52.639
After all, if your hero can already beat the
big baddie from the get go, then how do you

00:05:52.639 --> 00:05:53.979
build conflict?

00:05:53.979 --> 00:06:00.139
The answer – for shonen, at least – lies
in a three part formula for plot: pacing +

00:06:00.139 --> 00:06:03.069
information + side stories = conflict.

00:06:03.069 --> 00:06:04.599
So let’s break that formula down.

00:06:04.599 --> 00:06:09.479
Did you ever wonder why it took so many episodes
for Goku to get involved on Planet Namek?

00:06:09.479 --> 00:06:14.900
Sure, he was busy training at 100 times Earth’s
gravity so he could take on Frieza, but there’s

00:06:14.900 --> 00:06:18.370
another, much simpler reason: to build tension.

00:06:18.370 --> 00:06:22.729
See, for our overpowered heroes, there’s
little doubt in the audience’s mind that

00:06:22.729 --> 00:06:26.469
they can beat the villain from the start,
but that would make for some very quick and

00:06:26.469 --> 00:06:28.129
very boring TV.

00:06:28.129 --> 00:06:32.639
As such, shows need a way to delay the hero’s
entry – usually through a combination of

00:06:32.639 --> 00:06:37.069
clever pacing and what I like to call asymmetric
information.

00:06:37.069 --> 00:06:41.599
Asymmetric Information refers to information
that the audience, and oftentimes other characters,

00:06:41.599 --> 00:06:44.340
is privy to, but our main hero is not.

00:06:44.340 --> 00:06:48.860
So while Goku conveniently literally spends
19 episodes hurtling through space, blissfully

00:06:48.860 --> 00:06:53.280
unaware of what’s happening on Namek, the
audience is slowly growing more anxious as

00:06:53.280 --> 00:06:58.740
it watches Krillin and Gohan gather the dragonballs
and brush ever closer to Frieza.

00:06:58.740 --> 00:07:02.699
But in delaying your main hero’s arrival,
you’re arguably putting your main plotline

00:07:02.699 --> 00:07:06.169
– or your A story in TV-speak – on hold.

00:07:06.169 --> 00:07:10.280
As a consequence, shows have to really drum
up their B and C stories – more commonly

00:07:10.280 --> 00:07:13.300
known as side plots – in order to compensate.

00:07:13.300 --> 00:07:16.279
Basically if your main hero’s not doing
anything, then the rest of the cast needs

00:07:16.279 --> 00:07:17.919
to pick up the slack.

00:07:17.919 --> 00:07:22.050
In doing so, these B and C stories will keep
help drive the overall narrative forward,

00:07:22.050 --> 00:07:26.559
letting the hero swoop in at the last minute
and bring the main plotline to a close.

00:07:26.559 --> 00:07:30.569
Keeping with our DBZ example, we spend most
of our time on Namek following two groups:

00:07:30.569 --> 00:07:34.830
Gohan, Krillin, and Bulma in one group, and
Vegeta in another.

00:07:34.830 --> 00:07:38.289
These two groups make up our B and C plots,
respectively.

00:07:38.289 --> 00:07:42.800
Gohan and co. are looking for the Dragon Balls
to revive their dead friends, while simultaneously

00:07:42.800 --> 00:07:46.619
trying to save the last of the Namek people
from Frieza’s ruthless slaughter.

00:07:46.619 --> 00:07:51.229
Vegeta on the other hand is searching for
the Dragon Balls to receive immortality, thereby

00:07:51.229 --> 00:07:55.689
guaranteeing that he can kill Frieza- the
guy who destroyed his planet.

00:07:55.689 --> 00:07:59.949
And it’s no coincidence that where these
two plotlines come to a point is also when

00:07:59.949 --> 00:08:01.409
Goku arrives.

00:08:01.409 --> 00:08:06.229
Under attack by Frieza’s elite mercenary
brigade, the two groups form an uneasy alliance

00:08:06.229 --> 00:08:11.240
– agreeing to use one wish to make Vegeta
immortal, and the other two to revive Gohan’s

00:08:11.240 --> 00:08:12.240
dead friends.

00:08:12.240 --> 00:08:16.240
But just when things couldn’t be any worse
– with the gang beaten half to death and

00:08:16.240 --> 00:08:21.719
all 7 Dragon Balls in Frieza’s possession
– who should finally land on Namek but our

00:08:21.719 --> 00:08:22.719
hero?

00:08:22.719 --> 00:08:28.720
From there, it’s straightforward march right
to Frieza vs. Goku, and the rest is history.

00:08:28.720 --> 00:08:34.349
And sure, this one-two punch of pacing and
asymmetric information might seem cheap, but

00:08:34.349 --> 00:08:37.479
it’s the bread and butter of animes with
OP characters.

00:08:37.479 --> 00:08:42.149
Case in point, Season 1 of One-Punch Man is
a masterclass of this technique.

00:08:42.149 --> 00:08:47.230
Regardless of who the baddy is, Saitama’s
only enters the fray at the last moment, usually

00:08:47.230 --> 00:08:50.120
as we watch characters like Genos take a beating.

00:08:50.120 --> 00:08:53.690
We can see this happening here
and here... andddd here...

00:08:53.690 --> 00:08:59.040
But where I think Season 1 pulled this technique
off best is in the Deep Sea King arc.

00:08:59.040 --> 00:09:08.970
In it, well, these guys show up: After watching
several A-Class Heroes and even a few S-Class

00:09:08.970 --> 00:09:13.040
ones bite the dust, Genos and Saitama decide
to rush to the rescue.

00:09:13.040 --> 00:09:18.430
Of course, Genos goes to scout scene first,
leaving Saitama in the dark – which is simultaneously

00:09:18.430 --> 00:09:35.050
how the show controls the pacing and creates
the asymmetric information for the arc.

00:09:35.050 --> 00:09:39.550
While Saitama wanders around for nearly two
episodes completely clueless, the tension

00:09:39.550 --> 00:09:43.560
is left to boil through the rising stakes
of the B and C stories.

00:09:43.560 --> 00:09:47.829
First, One-Punch Man sets the stage by letting
us watch the Sea King get increasingly more

00:09:47.829 --> 00:09:53.029
powerful in the rain, even beating Speed-o’-Sound
Sonic- ya know, the guy Saitama accidentally

00:09:53.029 --> 00:09:54.310
punched in the dick.

00:09:54.310 --> 00:09:58.930
The stakes then skyrocket as the Deep Sea
King stumbles upon a whole stadium full of

00:09:58.930 --> 00:10:02.339
evacuees, vowing to kill each and every one
of them.

00:10:02.339 --> 00:10:05.380
That is, before our B plot arrives in the
form of Genos.

00:10:05.380 --> 00:10:10.370
While the two face off in an increasing arms
race of abilities, Genos ultimately loses

00:10:10.370 --> 00:10:13.360
when he sacrifices himself to protect an innocent
bystander.

00:10:13.360 --> 00:10:18.209
A ball of acid later and Genos is left looking
like this…

00:10:18.209 --> 00:10:23.050
As all hope seems lost, though, our C plot
enters the fray – Mumen Rider.

00:10:23.050 --> 00:10:27.480
Despite being downright mediocre, the show
painstakingly crafts an arc for this loveable

00:10:27.480 --> 00:10:28.480
hero.

00:10:28.480 --> 00:10:32.860
The first episode of the Deep Sea King arc
opens with this no-name hero worrying about

00:10:32.860 --> 00:10:43.930
Saitama’s recent rise in the ranks
Then, when the threat level gets raised from

00:10:43.930 --> 00:10:52.589
Tiger to Demon, it shows Mumen Rider refusing
to run away like all the other C Class heroes.

00:10:52.589 --> 00:11:09.350
All of

00:11:09.350 --> 00:11:14.759
this leads to an amazing climax, when against
all common sense, Mumen Rider – whose only

00:11:14.759 --> 00:11:19.540
ability is that he rides a bicycle – tries
to take on the Deep Sea King himself.

00:11:19.540 --> 00:11:25.259
It’s a powerful scene – one that contains
all of One-Punch Man’s love for genre inversions,

00:11:25.259 --> 00:11:32.160
right down to that horrible pause when it’s
clear that Mumen Rider isn’t up to the task

00:11:32.160 --> 00:11:36.829
But just like when Goku arrives on Namek,
Saitama finally arrives just as the B and

00:11:36.829 --> 00:11:40.980
C plots come to a head.

00:11:40.980 --> 00:11:45.670
In holding the hero back until the final moment,
the show manages to drive the tension up to

00:11:45.670 --> 00:11:51.709
a crisis point, before using our boy, Bald
Cape, to resolve it all with one final punch.

00:11:51.709 --> 00:11:56.199
Now, structurally, you might be thinking that
Season 2 has done the same thing, and I’ll

00:11:56.199 --> 00:11:58.100
grant you that it does- sometimes.

00:11:58.100 --> 00:12:02.560
Let’s look at the season’s main arc, for
example, the Martial Arts Tournament.

00:12:02.560 --> 00:12:06.360
In it, Saitama impersonates Silver Fang’s
student, Chiranko, in order to learn what

00:12:06.360 --> 00:12:09.480
true martial arts is and take on the Hero
Hunter, Garo.

00:12:09.480 --> 00:12:14.149
Of course, the series doesn’t just propel
Saitama to the finals, and we spend most of

00:12:14.149 --> 00:12:18.769
the episodes either watching Saitama instant
KO lower ranking opponents, or hear him talk

00:12:18.769 --> 00:12:28.540
with his new buddy, Nigamushi.

00:12:28.540 --> 00:12:33.269
And sure, the arc ends with a halfway decent
fight scene between Saitama and Suiryu, complete

00:12:33.269 --> 00:12:51.690
with a typical One Punch Man inversion – Saitama’s
complete misunderstanding of martial arts.

00:12:51.690 --> 00:12:56.040
Given all these similarities, you’d expect
this arc to work, but it doesn’t – and

00:12:56.040 --> 00:13:00.470
it all comes back to pacing, information,
and your B and C Stories.

00:13:00.470 --> 00:13:03.360
So let’s step back and take a broader view
of this season.

00:13:03.360 --> 00:13:07.320
As it turns out, a large part of why this
arc falls so dramatically flat is because

00:13:07.320 --> 00:13:10.220
Saitama’s story is not the A plot.

00:13:10.220 --> 00:13:15.800
Unlike the Deep Sea King arc, Saitama’s
quest to experience martial arts isn’t what’s

00:13:15.800 --> 00:13:17.940
driving the larger story forward.

00:13:17.940 --> 00:13:22.740
In fact, outside of the arena, we see a brand
new Monsters’ Association rise up, wreak

00:13:22.740 --> 00:13:27.571
all kinds of havoc, and take a hostage – all
with the aim of putting an end to the Hero

00:13:27.571 --> 00:13:29.519
Association as we know it.

00:13:29.519 --> 00:13:33.970
In this sense, Genos’ and the other heroes’
struggle against this new group forms the

00:13:33.970 --> 00:13:38.389
true meat of our larger narrative – our
A plot if you will.

00:13:38.389 --> 00:13:43.399
And sure, we’re 100% willing to grant that
this arc employs some clever use of pacing

00:13:43.399 --> 00:13:47.949
and asymmetric information in order to keep
Saitama from entering the fray.

00:13:47.949 --> 00:13:51.940
After all, because Saitama is locked into
the tournament, he has absolutely no clue

00:13:51.940 --> 00:13:55.920
about what’s going on outside the arena,
leaving others characters like Genos to clean

00:13:55.920 --> 00:14:03.339
up the mess.

00:14:03.339 --> 00:14:14.879
However, Season 2 doesn’t try to connect
Saitama to this larger storyline, in essence

00:14:14.879 --> 00:14:17.380
relegating our boy, Bald Cape to the B plot.

00:14:17.380 --> 00:14:21.899
In fact, his fight against Suiryu and the
rest of the martial arts nobodies is less

00:14:21.899 --> 00:14:26.900
connected to the A Plot than the C Plot – Garo’s
quest to become a monster.

00:14:26.900 --> 00:14:45.089
And at least Garo is aware of, and continually
approached by, the Monster’s Association:

00:14:45.089 --> 00:14:46.089
.

00:14:46.089 --> 00:14:49.350
As a result, instead of watching our side
stories build to a crescendo for our main

00:14:49.350 --> 00:14:52.610
storyline, we’re left feeling that our main
hero did nothing.

00:14:52.610 --> 00:14:57.890
Sure, Genos is smashed to pieces and things
seem bleak, but for what?

00:14:57.890 --> 00:15:02.470
In essence, none of our storylines for the
bulk of Season 2 are working in concert with

00:15:02.470 --> 00:15:07.279
one another, creating a fractured narrative
that never truly builds to a climax.

00:15:07.279 --> 00:15:12.180
Of course, there is one bright spot in Season
2 that works as a stand-alone arc.

00:15:12.180 --> 00:15:17.029
One where the shonen formula is properly deploys,
and where the arc’s specific A, B, and C

00:15:17.029 --> 00:15:21.690
plots properly gel – even if the A, B, and
C’s of Season 2 don’t.

00:15:21.690 --> 00:15:25.519
I’m talking about the two episodes following
Saitama’s fight with Suiryu.

00:15:25.519 --> 00:15:29.860
In it, the Monsters Association barges into
the arena, demanding the martial artists join

00:15:29.860 --> 00:15:34.410
their ranks by eating monster cells.

00:15:34.410 --> 00:15:43.400
However, Saitama, fearing what the tournament
officials will do after his cover is blown,

00:15:43.400 --> 00:15:44.899
has long since escaped.

00:15:44.899 --> 00:15:48.810
Instead, for the entirety of the first episode,
the audience watches Suiryu and the other

00:15:48.810 --> 00:15:54.189
martial artists fight back against Goketsu,
the very same monster that nearly killed Genos.

00:15:54.189 --> 00:15:57.160
Already, we can see the shonen formula in
action.

00:15:57.160 --> 00:15:59.820
Controlling the pace via Saitama’s delayed
entry?

00:15:59.820 --> 00:16:00.820
Check.

00:16:00.820 --> 00:16:02.540
Upping the stakes through asymmetric information?

00:16:02.540 --> 00:16:03.540
Check.

00:16:03.540 --> 00:16:07.480
But we can also see the A, B, and C plots
finally building on one another.

00:16:07.480 --> 00:16:11.220
First, we watch the B plot, which focuses
on Suiryu’s growth.

00:16:11.220 --> 00:16:14.449
When first introduced to this world class
martial artist, we were pretty much led to

00:16:14.449 --> 00:16:16.009
believe that the guy’s a dick.

00:16:16.009 --> 00:16:19.790
The only reason he wants to be strong is so
he can coast through life, and his views on

00:16:19.790 --> 00:16:28.459
heroes are, well, less than stellar: .

00:16:28.459 --> 00:16:37.170
However, as we watch other martial artists
give into the temptation of the Monsters’

00:16:37.170 --> 00:16:56.249
Association, Suiryu endears himself to us
by remaining firm.

00:16:56.249 --> 00:17:00.769
And with the help of our C Plot – the selfless
arrival of the two heroes Suiryu mercilessly

00:17:00.769 --> 00:17:05.790
defeated in tournament, Lightning Max and
Snek, we watch Suiryu gain a new respect for

00:17:05.790 --> 00:17:06.929
our heroes.

00:17:06.929 --> 00:17:11.170
Of course, the point of the B and C storylines
is to build tension for our main hero and

00:17:11.170 --> 00:17:14.810
the A plot, which is exactly what happens
here.

00:17:14.810 --> 00:17:19.070
The situation first goes from bad to worse
when Snek and Lightning Max are defeated,

00:17:19.070 --> 00:17:23.580
and takes another nosedive when the martial
artists Bakuzan successfully transforms into

00:17:23.580 --> 00:17:26.870
a monster, blocking Suiryu’s escape.

00:17:26.870 --> 00:17:41.309
Left on the verge of death, Suiryu completes
his arc by finally calling out for

00:17:41.309 --> 00:17:53.990
a hero himself.

00:17:53.990 --> 00:18:14.500
And of course, this is when our hero, and
the A Plot, finally arrives.

00:18:14.500 --> 00:18:18.260
One short punch later and it’s over.

00:18:18.260 --> 00:18:21.309
But here’s the thing.

00:18:21.309 --> 00:18:26.120
While those two episodes really captured what
made Season 1 so great, it was just too little

00:18:26.120 --> 00:18:27.270
too late.

00:18:27.270 --> 00:18:31.100
As an audience member, it’s just plain boring
to watch your hero linger on the sidelines

00:18:31.100 --> 00:18:36.380
for over half a season, only to have them
briefly occupy the spotlight for two episodes.

00:18:36.380 --> 00:18:40.960
While Season 1 regularly built its episodes
with Saitama at its center, always using the

00:18:40.960 --> 00:18:46.330
shonen formula to drive tension to a max,
Season 2 largely misses the mark.

00:18:46.330 --> 00:18:48.059
And maybe this is intentional.

00:18:48.059 --> 00:18:52.010
If there’s one series that’s known for
subverting tropes, it’s One-Punch Man, and

00:18:52.010 --> 00:18:56.750
maybe Season 2 amounts to a conscious rejection
of the very Shonen formula that worked so

00:18:56.750 --> 00:18:58.120
well in Season 1.

00:18:58.120 --> 00:19:02.650
In fact, sometimes it feels like this is downright
the case, like when Saitama finds out all

00:19:02.650 --> 00:19:13.580
the monsters retreated 30 minutes before he
arrived on the scene.

00:19:13.580 --> 00:19:17.130
But maybe some conventions shouldn’t be
thrown out the window.

00:19:17.130 --> 00:19:21.470
With Genos being rushed off to be repaired
and the Hero Association preparing for war,

00:19:21.470 --> 00:19:26.390
the A Plot and any role Saitama can play in
it is banished to the background.

00:19:26.390 --> 00:19:34.500
Instead, Saitama returns to… well, playing
videogames with King.

00:19:34.500 --> 00:19:45.210
Of course, this is part of the joke.

00:19:45.210 --> 00:19:49.010
Rather than training for the ultimate battle,
Siatama is getting increasingly frustrated

00:19:49.010 --> 00:19:53.460
by losing to King, and only takes a brief
break to defeat the Elder Centipede with a

00:19:53.460 --> 00:19:54.740
single punch.

00:19:54.740 --> 00:19:57.299
It’s not that this isn’t funny- it is.

00:19:57.299 --> 00:20:02.140
It’s that whereas this was once once a show
ABOUT Saitama, it now feels like he’s merely

00:20:02.140 --> 00:20:05.700
appended at the end of whatever arc needs
to find completion.

00:20:05.700 --> 00:20:09.750
He’s now more of a plot element than a character.

00:20:09.750 --> 00:20:11.200
But what do you think, Wisecrack?

00:20:11.200 --> 00:20:15.779
Is the main problem with Season 2 the show’s
inability to execute the same Shonen formula

00:20:15.779 --> 00:20:17.870
that made Season 1 so great?

00:20:17.870 --> 00:20:21.789
Or is it something simpler, like the joke
has simply run on too long?

00:20:21.789 --> 00:20:25.430
Maybe we’re tired of all spoofs on the world’s
strongest hero?

00:20:25.430 --> 00:20:28.520
Whatever your take, remember to drop us a
line in the comments section and let us know.

00:20:28.520 --> 00:20:31.640
Thanks to all our patrons who support the
channel and our podcasts.

00:20:31.640 --> 00:20:52.980
Don’t forget to hit that subscribe button,
and as always, peace.

